  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Acoustic monitoring in the Republic of Congo: establishing expert capacity</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project supports capacity development at The Cornell Elephant Listening Project for an advanced field biologist, Mr. Clement Inkamba-Nkulu of the Republic of Congo. It will enhance his expertise on acoustic monitoring tools for elephant conservation. The project is intended to conserve forest elephants by improving the ability to track changes in elephant activity in dense forested landscapes, estimate population density and quantify human activities such as hunting and their impact on elephant movements and behavior. Specific activities include: 1) providing in-depth training in sound analysis and visualization; 2) ensuring a level of training that enables independent acoustic monitoirng research and the ability to train other field biologists and technicians in these skills; and 3) analyzing archived acoustic data and preparing a manuscript that interprets these results.</narrative>
  </description>
